WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.472
Welcome to Module 6, Joining Data in PostgreSQL.

00:00:04.472 --> 00:00:10.102
Up to this point, many of our examples have focused on one table at a time.

00:00:10.102 --> 00:00:14.372
That is useful for learning SQL, but real projects rarely stay inside one table.

00:00:14.372 --> 00:00:21.862
A hospital may store patient demographics in one table, admissions in another, lab tests in another,

00:00:21.862 --> 00:00:23.612
and staff information somewhere else.

00:00:23.612 --> 00:00:29.072
A university may separate students, courses, registrations, and grades.

00:00:29.072 --> 00:00:35.372
An online store may separate customers, orders, products, and payments.

00:00:35.372 --> 00:00:38.237
To answer useful questions, we often need to bring those pieces together.

00:00:38.237 --> 00:00:43.377
The important skill in this module is not just memorizing JOIN syntax.

00:00:43.377 --> 00:00:48.507
I want you to think about what the final rows mean.

00:00:48.507 --> 00:00:54.277
If one patient has three admissions, should that patient appear once or three times?

00:00:54.277 --> 00:00:59.757
If a patient has no admissions, should the patient disappear or remain?

00:00:59.757 --> 00:01:02.367
Those questions determine the join strategy.

00:01:02.367 --> 00:01:09.327
This same reasoning applies whether you are joining hospital data, customer data, student records,

00:01:09.327 --> 00:01:10.567
or financial transactions.

00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:17.992
We will work with INNER, LEFT, RIGHT, and FULL OUTER joins, but we will also go beyond the basic definitions.

00:01:17.992 --> 00:01:21.522
We will compare ON and WHERE, use self joins and cross joins,

00:01:21.522 --> 00:01:29.502
and spend time on cardinality because row multiplication can quietly create incorrect counts.

00:01:29.502 --> 00:01:34.172
As you move through the module, try to predict the output before you run the SQL.

00:01:34.172 --> 00:01:38.802
Then run the query and compare your prediction with the result.

00:01:38.802 --> 00:01:47.292
That habit is extremely useful in real analytics work because a query can run successfully and still answer

00:01:47.292 --> 00:01:54.657
the wrong question. In a real project, a wrong join can change a dashboard, a report, or even a decision,

00:01:54.657 --> 00:02:01.549
so understanding the meaning of the rows is more important than simply getting a green execution message.
